  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Zenodo software release shows clear early use, with 296 downloads and 1,285 views recorded. It is openly accessible and linked to a versioned GitHub release, but the scholarly record is still sparse: OpenAlex currently shows no citations, and there is no scite evidence or recorded online discussion in the available sources.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">stineb/rpmodel: Stocker et al. (2019) GMD, SUBMISSION2 is a software deposit by Benjamin Stocker at ETH Zurich, archived in Zenodo as an open, citable research object. The record identifies it as a software resource and connects it to a specific GitHub release, which supports reproducibility and version tracking.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest signal in the current record is usage. Zenodo reports 1,285 views and 296 downloads, indicating that the release has attracted measurable attention and has been accessed repeatedly. For a software tool, this kind of usage evidence is an important sign of reach, especially when citation-based indicators are still absent.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the scholarly impact profile remains early. OpenAlex currently shows 0 citations, and scite records no citing, supporting, or contradicting mentions. That means the available evidence does not yet show documented scholarly reuse or critical discussion in the indexed literature, though the absence of citations should be read as a snapshot of the current record rather than a judgment on eventual influence.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Stewardship signals are strong: the deposit is preserved in Zenodo, a repository with open database access and DOI-based persistence, and OpenAIRE reports the item as openly available in two open instances. The record also includes a related identifier linking to the software release, reinforcing the connection between the archived version and its development history. Overall, this is a well-curated software archive with demonstrable use and a still-limited citation footprint.</p>",
